Q: Why is &#39;&#39; !== false? A: <p>The !== operator checks if the <strong>type</strong> and the <strong>value</strong> of the variables are the same.
The != operator checks if only the value is the same.</p>
<p>'' and false are equals the same in terms of value in js, but '' is a string and false is a boolean value.</p>
<p>If you want to get <strong>2</strong> as return, you need to change your code to:</p>
<pre><code>emptyWord = '';

if (emptyWord != false) {
    console.log(1);
} else {
    console.log(2);
}
</code></pre>
<p>('' !== false) is true.
('' != false) is false.</p>

